00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:31.580
which leads us neatly into today's strategy spotlight. The framework we're examining this morning

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:38.520
is commodity input cost pass-through as a margin protection strategy. At its core, this approach looks

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:44.080
for companies that have demonstrated the ability to quickly and credibly pass higher raw material,

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.920
logistics, or energy costs onto their customers through contractual escalators,

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:55.680
automatic fuel surcharges, or transparent price increases. When executed well, it protects gross margins,

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:00.760
and in some cases even expands them during periods of inflationary supply shocks.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:06.660
We're seeing this play out in real time. News this week that maple leaf foods and several other

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:13.520
major Canadian food processors are implementing fuel cost surcharges of up to $2,220,000 kilogram

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:19.860
tractor trailer load is a textbook example. Elevated diesel prices stemming from the Hormuz constraints

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:25.540
and broader energy volatility have pushed transportation costs materially higher. Rather than simply

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:30.620
absorbing the hit to margins, these companies are explicitly passing it downstream. That's exactly the

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:35.680
behavior we want to identify and systematically screen for. Here's how Canadian investors can put

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:41.760
this into practice on the TSX. Start by building a focused watch list within consumer staples,

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:48.980
industrials, and certain specialty chemicals names. Use SEDAR plus to pull the most recent management

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:56.440
discussion and analysis, MD&amp;A sections, and earnings call transcripts. Search for explicit language

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:02.820
around fuel adjustment mechanisms, cost escalators, surcharge clauses, or pass-through pricing.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:08.840
Once you've narrowed the list, compare gross margins stability over the past two volatile diesel

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:14.440
quarters against industry peers who lack those contractual protections. The names that have held

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:20.960
gross margins within 50 to 80 basis points of their three-year average while peers compressed by 200

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:26.680
basis points or more are the ones worth deeper fundamental work. Historically, this strategy has

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:31.220
performed best in the three to nine-month window following major energy price spikes.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:37.000
Precisely the window where input costs stay elevated but end-demand remains relatively

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:42.400
inelastic. Of course, there are risks. Customers can push back hard enough that companies are forced

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:47.840
to absorb some of the cost, or regulators can step in and cap allowable surcharges. That's why we

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:53.580
don't treat this as a set it and forget it idea. It requires ongoing monitoring of both commodity

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:59.460
benchmarks and company-specific pricing power. Practically, you can do a lot of this work with free or

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:05.680
low-cost tools. Cedar plus for filings, wealth simple or quest-rate screeners with custom filters

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:11.780
on gross margin delta versus the Bloomberg diesel or WTI benchmarks, and even simple Excel tracking

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:16.700
of quarterly margin trends. The beauty is that once you've built the screen, it becomes a repeatable

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:22.340
process. You can run every time energy markets spike. Exactly the kind of repeatable edge that

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:28.060
separates serious investors from those who simply react to headlines. Protecting margins during

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:33.060
cost shocks is critical, but it's only one part of the equation. You also have to protect yourself

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:37.860
from getting picked off in the execution phase, especially in thin headline-driven tapes.

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.700
That brings us to a topic many of you have asked me about in the DMs and comments sections,

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:48.140
adverse selection and order book toxicity. Let me paint a picture that will feel painfully familiar

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:54.160
to some of you. Imagine you see the US Iran ceasefire headlines hit the wires, oil jumps,

00:06:54.160 --> 00:07:00.260
and you decide to jump into an energy services name with a market order for 500 shares. Your order

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:09.220
fills at $18.42. 90 seconds later, the stock is trading at $18.19, and you're already down 1.25%

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:14.240
with nothing fundamentally negative having happened. What actually occurred is adverse selection.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:19.480
High-frequency traders and institutional algorithms spotted your marketable order hitting a relatively

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:24.680
thin post-news tape and leaned against it. The result, the effective spread you paid,

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:31.160
ballooned from the displayed 4 cents to a realized 23 cents. In the current environment of geopolitically

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:36.920
driven oil volatility, we've seen average bid-asks spreads on mid-cap energy and mining names expand

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:42.780
by roughly 180% during the first 30 minutes after major headlines compared with quiet sessions.

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:48.040
The professional technique most retail investors still miss is to pause and read the order book

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:53.000
imbalance and relative volume ratio on a proper level 2 platform before you click buy.

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:59.680
When relative volume is running below 0.6 times the 20-day average, the pros almost always switch

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:05.700
from market orders to carefully placed limit orders or even a small VWAP algorithm. The biggest

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:09.780
misconception is believing that the quoted price on your screen is the price you will actually

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:16.480
receive. Instead, the discipline question to ask yourself is, is my order likely to be toxic to

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:21.120
the current order flow? If the answer is yes, you either reduce size, improve your limit,

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:27.300
wait for the first flush of headline volume to clear, or simply stand aside. Mastering this single

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:32.460
habit can save you dozens of basis points per trade, which compounds dramatically over a year.

